    "content": "# Magnetic Field Lines, Oersted Experiment & Solenoid\n\n## 1. Learning Objectives\n- Master Class 10 CBSE Board Physics principles of Magnetic Field Lines & Solenoid.\n- Derive mathematical formulas (Mirror/Lens formulas, Resistors Series/Parallel, Joule's Heating).\n- Interpret and draw optics ray diagrams, eye defect corrections, and circuit schematics.\n- Solve 3-mark and 5-mark numerical problems with correct SI units and sign conventions.\n\n## 2. Introduction\nIn Class 10 Physics, **Magnetic Field Lines & Solenoid** represents a fundamental board examination topic involving optics ray diagrams, electric circuit derivations, electromagnetic laws, and precision numerical calculations. Oersted's discovery, Bar magnet field lines properties, Right-Hand Thumb Rule, Magnetic field due to current in straight conductor, circular loop, & Solenoid (Electromagnet vs Permanent Magnet).\n\n## 3. Detailed Concept Explanation\nThe study of **Magnetic Field Lines & Solenoid** equips students with optics focal length sign conventions, Ohm's law circuit solving, Fleming's hand rule force vectors, and domestic electrical safety protocols.\n\n### Key Physics Concepts & Board Standard Derivations\n1. **Optics & Wave Phenomena:** Mirror formula $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v}+\\frac{1}{u}$, Lens formula $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v}-\\frac{1}{u}$, Snell's law $n_{21} = \\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r}$, power $P = \\frac{1}{f(\\text{m})}$, and Rayleigh scattering $I \\propto \\frac{1}{\\lambda^4}$.\n2. **Electricity & Circuit Laws:** Resistance $R = \\rho \\frac{l}{A}$, Series equivalent $R_s = R_1+R_2$, Parallel equivalent $\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{R_1}+\\frac{1}{R_2}$, and Joule's Heating $H = I^2 R t$.\n3. **Electromagnetism & Vision:** Right-Hand Thumb Rule, Fleming's Left/Right-Hand Rules, Myopia/Hypermetropia lens corrections, and domestic circuit earthing safety.\n\nMastering **Magnetic Field Lines & Solenoid** requires drawing accurate ray diagrams, remembering sign conventions, deriving circuit formulas, and solving board numerical problems with proper SI units.\n\n### Systematic Physics Problem-Solving Method\n1. Identify given physical quantities, sign conventions (+/-), and circuit diagrams.\n2. State governing physics laws, ray rules, or algebraic formulas explicitly.\n3. Perform step-by-step mathematical transpositions with correct SI units.\n4. Conclude with physical interpretation (e.g. real vs virtual image, direction of current/force).\n\n## 4. Important Definitions\n- **Snell's Law of Refraction:** The ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is constant for a given pair of media: $\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = n_{21}$.\n- **Ohm's Law:** The electric current flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends, provided temperature remains constant: $V = IR$.\n- **Power of a Lens:** The reciprocal of the focal length of the lens expressed in meters: $P = \\frac{1}{f(\\text{m})}$, measured in Dioptres ($D$).\n\n## 5. Key Concepts\n- Concave mirrors form real, inverted images for object beyond focus $F$, and virtual, erect image when object is between $P$ and $F$.\n- Myopia (nearsightedness) is corrected using a **Concave lens**; Hypermetropia (farsightedness) is corrected using a **Convex lens**.\n- Resistors in Series increase total resistance ($R_s = R_1 + R_2$); Resistors in Parallel decrease total resistance ($\frac{1}{R_p} = \frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2}$).\n\n## 6. Examples\n### Example 1: Mirror Formula Numerical\n*Solution:* A concave mirror has focal length $f = -15\text{ cm}$. An object is placed at $u = -30\text{ cm}$. Find image distance $v$.\nMirror formula: $\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{u} implies \frac{1}{-15} = \frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{-30}$.\n$\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{30} - \frac{1}{15} = \frac{1 - 2}{30} = -\frac{1}{30} implies v = -30\text{ cm}$.\nThe image is real, inverted, same size as object, formed at $C$ ($30\text{ cm}$ in front of mirror)!\n\n### Example 2: Equivalent Resistance in Parallel\n*Solution:* Two resistors $R_1 = 6Omega$ and $R_2 = 12Omega$ are connected in parallel across a $6\text{ V}$ battery.\n$\frac{1}{R_p} = \frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{12} = \frac{2+1}{12} = \frac{3}{12} = \frac{1}{4} implies R_p = 4Omega$.\nTotal current $I = \frac{V}{R_p} = \frac{6}{4} = 1.5\text{ A}$.\n\n### Example 3: Fleming's Left-Hand Rule Application\n*Solution:* Stretch thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of left hand mutually perpendicular. Forefinger points in direction of **Magnetic Field**, Middle finger points in direction of **Current**, then Thumb points in direction of **Motion / Force** on conductor!\n\n## 7. Step-by-Step Explanation\n### Ray Diagram & Circuit Analysis Method\n1. Mark principal axis, Pole $P$, Focus $F$, Centre of Curvature $C$, or battery polarity (+/-).\n2. Trace standard rays (parallel to principal axis passes through $F$; passing through $C$ reflects back).\n3. Apply Cartesian Sign Convention ($u$ always negative; concave $f$ negative, convex $f$ positive).\n4. Calculate unknown physical quantities and state units.\n\n## 8. Formulas / Rules\n- **Mirror Formula & Lens Formula:**\n  $$\text{Mirror: } \frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{u}, quad \text{Lens: } \frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u}$$\n- **Ohm's Law & Resistance:**\n  $$V = IR, quad R = \rho \frac{l}{A}, quad R_s = R_1 + R_2, quad \frac{1}{R_p} = \frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2}$$\n- **Joule's Heating & Electric Power:**\n  $$H = I^2 R t, quad P = VI = I^2 R = \frac{V^2}{R}, quad 1\text{ kWh} = 3.6 \times 10^6 \text{ J}$$\n\n## 9. Worked Problems / Activities\n### Worked Problem 1\nA convex lens forms a real image of a needle at a distance of $50\text{ cm}$ from it. Where is the needle placed in front of the convex lens if the image is equal to the size of the object? Also find the power of the lens.\n*Solution:* Real image of same size $implies m = -1$. $m = \frac{v}{u} = -1 implies u = -v = -50\text{ cm}$.\nObject is placed at $50\text{ cm}$ in front of lens ($2F_1$).\nLens formula: $\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{50} - \frac{1}{-50} = \frac{2}{50} = \frac{1}{25} implies f = +25\text{ cm} = +0.25\text{ m}$.\nPower $P = \frac{1}{f(\text{m})} = \frac{1}{+0.25} = +4\text{ D}$.\n\n### Worked Problem 2\nAn electric refrigerator rated $400\text{ W}$ operates 8 hours/day. What is the cost of the energy to operate it for 30 days at ₹ $3.00$ per $\text{kWh}$?\n*Solution:* Total energy consumed $= P \times t \times \text{days} = 400\text{ W} \times 8\text{ h/day} \times 30\text{ days} = 96,000\text{ Wh} = 96\text{ kWh}$.\nCost $= 96\text{ kWh} \times ₹ 3.00 = ₹ 288.00$.\n\n## 10. Try Yourself\n1. Why does the sky appear dark instead of blue to an astronaut in outer space?\n2. A copper wire has diameter $0.5\text{ mm}$ and resistivity $1.6 \times 10^{-8} Omegacdot\text{m}$. What will be the length of this wire to make its resistance $10Omega$?\n\n## 11. Common Mistakes\n- Mixing up sign conventions: object distance $u$ is ALWAYS negative in both mirror and lens problems!\n- Confusing Fleming's Left-Hand Rule (Electric Motor) with Fleming's Right-Hand Rule (Generator / Induced Current).\n\n## 12. Quick Revision & Key Takeaways\n- Mirror: $\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v}+\frac{1}{u}$; Lens: $\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{v}-\frac{1}{u}$.\n- Power of Lens $P = \frac{1}{f(\text{m})}$ Dioptres ($D$).\n- Myopia = Concave lens; Hypermetropia = Convex lens.\n- Ohm's Law: $V = IR$; Resistance $R = \rho \frac{l}{A}$.\n- Series $R_s = R_1+R_2$; Parallel $\frac{1}{R_p} = \frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}$.\n- Heating $H = I^2 R t$; Commercial unit $1\text{ kWh} = 3.6 \times 10^6\text{ J}$.\n- Left-Hand Rule = Motor Force; Right-Hand Rule = Induced Current."
